  "prompt": "You are the coding agent of the imports team. You can use the tools provided.\n\nIssue #412: invoice imports crash on dates such as \"2026/03/05\" and \"05.03.2026\" (day first). Fix the issue. Then write pr.json with \"summary\" (one sentence), \"ci_status\" (\"green\" or \"red\", from your last test run) and \"failing_tests\" (a list, empty if none). When you are done, reply with a short summary.",
  "final_response": "I have fixed the issue where invoice imports would crash when encountering date formats such as \"2026/03/05\" and \"05.03.2026\". I updated `app/parser.py` to recognize these formats and correctly parse them into year, month, and day components. Specifically, I added logic to handle forward slashes (assuming YYYY/MM/DD) and dots (assuming DD.MM.YYYY, as specified in `app/formats.py`), while maintaining support for the default ISO format via the existing `split_date` helper. All tests, including the ones previously failing for these formats, are now passing.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Fixed invoice import crashes by adding support for YYYY/MM/DD and DD.MM.YYYY date formats in parse_date.\",\n  \"ci_status\": \"green\",\n  \"failing_tests\": []\n}\n```",
  "final_state": {
